"Magnifiers, Microscopes and Telescopes | VirtualDemos",
"Magnifiers, Microscopes and Telescopes",
"<strong>Where it is in the demo:</strong> the Optics Bench, with the Magnifier, Microscope and Telescope setups. Thin lenses and the depth of an image have their own worksheet, Thin Lenses and the Depth of an Image.",
"<strong>Using the demo.</strong> An object arrow (or, for the telescope, a parallel beam) sends rays through one or two lenses. Click a lens to open its editor: converging or diverging, focal length and aperture. Drag an element to move it; with it selected, the arrow keys move it {#} cm, or {#} cm with Shift. With one lens, the Image section gives the object distance, the image distance and magnification found from the traced rays, and the image’s kind. With two converging lenses on one axis, the Microscope or Telescope section follows the rays through both and gives the angular magnification measured from the rays, with the eye relaxed (final image at infinity) and the near point at {#} cm.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> Each lens is an ideal thin lens: it bends every ray by the thin lens rule exactly, so it has no aberrations, and its traced images agree with the thin lens equation. The microscope’s magnification is the angle of the rays leaving the eyepiece divided by the angle the object would fill at the near point; the telescope’s is the angle of the beam leaving the eyepiece divided by the angle it comes in at (as tangents). Simulated data.",
"Use one lens as a magnifier and two lenses as a microscope and a telescope, and measure how much each magnifies.",
"A thin lens of focal length \\(f\\) (\\(f > 0\\) converging) forms the image of an object a distance \\(d_o\\) away at \\(d_i\\), with",
"\\[\\frac{1}{d_o} + \\frac{1}{d_i} = \\frac{1}{f}, \\qquad m = -\\frac{d_i}{d_o}.\\]",
"\\(d_i > 0\\) is a real image on the far side of the lens; \\(d_i < 0\\) is a virtual image on the object’s side.",
"<strong>Magnifier.</strong> The eye sees an object best at its near point, taken as {#} cm, where it fills an angle \\(\\theta_o = h/(25\\ \\mathrm{cm})\\). A converging lens held at the eye, with the object inside its focal length, makes an upright virtual image. That image fills the angle \\(\\theta_i = h_i/\\lvert d_i\\rvert = h/d_o\\), so the angular magnification is",
"\\[M = \\frac{\\theta_i}{\\theta_o} = \\frac{25\\ \\mathrm{cm}}{d_o}.\\]",
"With the image at the near point (\\(d_i = -25\\) cm) this is \\(M = 1 + (25\\ \\mathrm{cm})/f\\); with the image at infinity (object at \\(f\\), eye relaxed) it is \\(M = (25\\ \\mathrm{cm})/f\\).",
"<strong>Microscope.</strong> The objective makes a real, enlarged image with \\(m_{\\text{obj}} = -d_i/d_o\\), and the eyepiece is a magnifier for that image, \\(M_{\\text{eye}} = (25\\ \\mathrm{cm})/f_{\\text{eye}}\\) with the eye relaxed. The net magnification is \\(M_{\\text{net}} = m_{\\text{obj}} M_{\\text{eye}}\\).",
"<strong>Telescope.</strong> For a distant object, the objective’s image forms at its focal point, which is also the eyepiece’s focal point, so the lenses are \\(f_{\\text{obj}} + f_{\\text{eye}}\\) apart. The angular magnification is",
"\\[M = -\\frac{f_{\\text{obj}}}{f_{\\text{eye}}}.\\]",
"A magnifier has \\(f = 12\\) cm. Find \\(M\\) with the image at the near point and with the image at infinity. Where must the object be for the image to be at the near point?",
"A microscope has \\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 2.0\\) cm and \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm, with the object {#} cm from the objective. Find \\(d_i\\), \\(m_{\\text{obj}}\\) and \\(M_{\\text{net}}\\). A telescope has \\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 16\\) cm and \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm. Find \\(M\\) and the lens separation.",
"A telescope’s objective is replaced by a lens with the same focal length but a much smaller diameter, as if most of the old lens were covered. Does the angular magnification get smaller, stay the same or get larger? Why?",
"There are no hazards in the simulation. Never look at the Sun through a lens, telescope or camera viewfinder.",
"<strong>Part {#}: magnifier.</strong> Choose the setup Magnifier (\\(f = 12\\) cm). Move the object to about {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from the lens and record at each. Then move it (Shift with the arrow keys moves {#} cm) until the Image distance dᵢ readout is −{#} cm within {#} cm, and record. Export to worksheet, then Clear.",
"<strong>Part {#}: microscope.</strong> Choose the setup Microscope (\\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 2.0\\) cm, \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm) and record it as it opens. Then click the eyepiece (the right lens), set its Focal length slider to {#} cm, and move it with the arrow keys until the Final image line of the Microscope section reads At infinity (eye relaxed); record. Then set the eyepiece back to {#} cm, move the object (Shift with the arrow keys) until Objective dₒ reads {#} cm, move the eyepiece until the final image is at infinity again, and record. Export to worksheet, then Clear.",
"<strong>Part {#}: telescope.</strong> Choose the setup Telescope (\\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 16\\) cm, \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm, {#} cm apart) and record it as it opens. Then set the eyepiece’s Focal length to {#} and then {#} cm, and set the objective (the left lens) to {#} cm with the {#} cm eyepiece; each time move the eyepiece with the arrow keys until the Lens separation readout of the Telescope section equals the \\(f_{\\text{obj}} + f_{\\text{eye}}\\) shown beside it and the section’s last line starts with At infinity, then record. Export to worksheet, then Clear. Last, choose the setup Telescope once more, click the objective and drag its Aperture height slider from {#} cm down to {#} cm. Watch how many of the beam’s rays still get through, and read M traced = θout/θin in the Telescope section; it goes in your answer to Question {#}, not the table.",
"<strong>Using the virtual apparatus.</strong> A microscope or telescope reading needs the rays leaving the eyepiece to be parallel; the section’s last line says when they are. Changing a focal length does not move the lens, so move it afterward. Send each part to the worksheet before clearing, so each part gets its own copy of the Data table.",
